\begin_layout Paragraph
Knowledge Persistence Behavior Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the higher-dimensional Hopper environment,
 agent persistence can be explained by two non-exclusive mechanisms.
 First,
 it is possible that agents trained under earlier gravity values retain partial competence in overlapping regions of the state-action space and adapt incrementally adapt as new data arrives.
 It would correspond to 
\series bold
knowledge reuse
\series default
.
 In this case the growth of agents can be explained mainly by the modeling complexity introduced by the change in dynamics.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Second,
 high-dimensional effects may stretch the Mahalanobis distance,
 yielding sparse activation of older agents that prevents both their meaningful adaption and confidence degradation.
 In this case,
 those agents become frozen in previously visited regions that do not overlap with newly discovered ones.
 it would correspond to 
\series bold
non-overlapping dynamics profiles
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To disambiguate whether agent persistence arises from adaptive knowledge reuse or from limited cross-regime activation,
 the activation patterns of the final agent population can be analyzed.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Heatmap-of-activations-hopper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presents a heatmap of agent activations for all training samples,
 where columns correspond to agents sorted by age (from oldest to youngest 
\begin_inset Formula $=$
\end_inset

 from left to right in the plot) and rows correspond to training data points aggregated into bins (from first seen points to last 
\begin_inset Formula $=$
\end_inset

 from top to bottom in the plot).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The heatmap exhibits distinct block patterns where older agents are primarily activated for samples from the earliest datasets.
 The shape of the agent populatio growth curve (seen previously in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Visualization-of-nb-agents-hopper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) is clearly visible in the heatmap,
 defined by the points where agents are most activated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This organization indicates that agents remain selectively active for the specific dynamics under which they were created.
 Rather than becoming obsolete,
 they retain a specialized expertise for their original corresponding dynamics.
 on which they were trained on and created.
\end_layout

