\begin_layout Subsubsection
Comparison between GPU and Tree Selection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The choice of using a geospatial index or GPU parallelization depends on the number of input dimensions and the quantity of agents required for learning the function underlying the data which is highly correlated to its complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To assesss the efficiency of each of the two approaches,
 we benchmark neighborhood selection in Python using 
\emph on
PyTorch
\emph default
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "paszke2019pytorch"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for GPU-accelerated computations and the 
\emph on
Rtree
\emph default
 package 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "gilliesRtreeSpatialIndexing"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for efficient R-tree indexing.
 Both selection strategies are compared on a synthetic nearest-neighbor selection task,
 varying both the number of input dimensions and the number of agents.
 As discussed in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 in kCELL,
 the local expert agents are self-organizing according to the encountered data.
 In real-world scenarios,
 these data points are rarely uniformly distributed.
 Thereore,
 a set of 100 clusters of synthetic agent centroids is generated to emulate high-density manifolds.
 Compared to uniform sampling which may exaggerate the effective search volume,
 generating a clustered distribution can allow for a more balanced comparison,
 especially for R-trees whose main advantage lies in efficiently pruning empty spaces.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
To evaluate the impact of this structural shift,
 we benchmark neighborhood selection in Python using 
\emph on
PyTorch
\emph default
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "paszke2019pytorch"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for GPU accelerated computations and using 
\emph on
Rtree
\emph default
 package 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "gilliesRtreeSpatialIndexing"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for efficient R-tree implementation.
 We compare the two selection approaches presented previously on a synthetic nearest neighbor selection task for variable amount of features and agents.
 The synthetic centroids are generated to form 100 clusters to simulate high density manifolds.
 As shown in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 in CELL instantiations like oCELL and kCELL,
 agents organize spatially according to the data encountered.
 In real-world cases,
 these data points are rarely uniformly distributed.
 Indeed,
 uniform distribution could overestimate the volume of search.
 Using clustered centroid distribution allows a fairer comparison for R-trees whose main strength is to prune empty space.
\end_layout

\end_inset


