 some agents appear elongated along one dimension especially for the Distance-based strategy.
 Analysis of their covariance matrices reveals that these agents correspond to sharp directional changes in the dynamics,
 typically induced by large control inputs applied against the direction of motion.
 As such,
 agent geometry seems to provide an interpretable signature of local dynamical regimes encountered during exploration.
 More of this kind of agents can be observed for the Distance-based strategy.
 This could be due to a more agressive exploration strategy that prioritizes more extreme action variations.
 These activation patterns demonstrate that kCELL does not only covered the state space uniformly but organized local models in a manner that reflects the underlying geometry and variability of the dynamics encountered during exploration.
\end_layout

