\begin_layout Standard
Traditionally,
 in multiagent systems,
 heterogeneity refers to a mixture of disting agent classes.
 For example,
 some agents would employ simple linear regressions while others would use high-order polynomials or neural networks.
 Such approach offers theoretical flexibility but introduces a significant meta-learning challenge as the system must decide a priori or through complex heuristics which model type is best suited for a specific locality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To bypass the need for manual model selection while retaining the benefits of diverse local knowledge representation,
 we propose a novel instantiation of CELL leveraging Gaussian Processes (GPs) as the internal model for each agent:
 gpCELL.
 By using the GPs ability to act as universal function approximators,
 the expressive power of each agent is not fixed,
 but emerges from its local training distribution.
 This approach not only allows agent to naturally adapt to varying local data complexities but also introduces an estimation of predictive uncertainty that is complementary to the epistemic uncertainty derived from the ensemble of local expert agents spatial organization (cf.
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The contributions presented in this chapter are threefold:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Scalable Online Gaussian Processes
\series default
:
 We introduce gpCELL,
 a novel CELL instantiation that bypasses the 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(n^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 computational bottleneck of global GPs through a horizontally scaling,
 distributed architecture.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Uncertainty Quantification in CELL
\series default
:
 The integration of GPs in a CELL instantiation allows for a more rigorous estimation of predictive uncertainty.
 We demonstrate through an online modeling task that this uncertainty is empirically well-calibrated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Heterogeneous Agent Design
\series default
:
 Through model selection,
 gpCELL allows each agent to independently adapt its complexity to the local data manifold.
 Thus,
 it represents a step towards the design of learning systems that leverage an heterogeneous ensemble of local expert agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Framework for Online GPs
\series default
:
 leveraging the CELL principles,
 gpCELL provides a scalable method for learning Gaussian Processes from a stream of data.
 By distribution the computational load across the population of local expert agents,
 the algorithm avoids the 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(n^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 bottleneck of global GPs,
 replacing it with a horizontally scaling architecture.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Gaussian Process Regression
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Gaussian-Process-Regression"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) is a Bayesian non-parametric framework used for supervised learning.
 Unlike traditional regression models that estimate fixed parameters for a specific function,
 GPR provides a 
\series bold
probabilistic distribution over possible functions
\series default
 that are plausible regarding the observed data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
GPR is widely used for modeling complex stochastic phenomena where uncertainty quantification is as critical as the prediction itself,
 such as in signal processing 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "perez2013gaussian"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 in financial modeling 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "gonzalvez2019financial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 or in dynamics modeling for safe control 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "berkenkamp2015safe"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the main advantages of Gaussian Processes (GPs) is that,
 with an appropriate choice of kernel (e.g.
 RBF kernel),
 they can represent arbitrarily complex continuous functions 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "williams2006gaussian"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 capturing any variation regime from highly irregular trajectories to smooth differentiable paths.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In GPR,
 the underlying function 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 is assumed to follow a GP prior,
 defined by a function 
\begin_inset Formula $m\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 (often assumed to be 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 or constant) and a covariance kernel 
\begin_inset Formula $K\left(x,x^{\prime}\right)$
\end_inset

 such as:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f\left(x\right)\sim\mathcal{GP}\left(m\left(x\right),K\left(x,x^{\prime}\right)\right)
\]

\end_inset

For a supervised learning task with training inputs 
\begin_inset Formula $X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}$
\end_inset

 and noisy observations 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

,
 assuming a zero mean prior,
 the joint distribution of the training data and the prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x_{*}\right)$
\end_inset

 at a new test point 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

,
 is a multivariate Gaussian vector:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\begin{array}{c}
y\\
f\left(x_{*}\right)
\end{array}\right)\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,\left(\begin{array}{cc}
K\left(X,X\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I & K\left(X,x_{*}\right)\\
K\left(x_{*},X\right) & K\left(x_{*},x_{*}\right)
\end{array}\right)\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{n}^{2}$
\end_inset

 represents the observation noise variance.
 The prediction is performed by conditioning this joint distribution on the observed data 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

,
 resulting in a posterior distribution for 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x_{*}\right)$
\end_inset

 defined by:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Predictive Mean
\series default
:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu_{*}=K\left(x_{*},X\right)\left[K\left(X,X\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I\right]^{-1}y
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Predictive Variance
\series default
:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sigma_{*}^{2}=K\left(x_{*},x_{*}\right)-K\left(x_{*},X\right)\left[K\left(X,X\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I\right]^{-1}K\left(X,x_{*}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Learning in GPR does not involve finding specific weights but rather 
\series bold
optimizing the hyperparameters
\series default
 of the kernel 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 This is usually achieved by maximizing the 
\series bold
log marginal likelihood
\series default
,
 which balances the model's fit to the training data against its complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite their flexibility,
 GPs have significant constraints:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Computational Complexity
\series default
:
 The inversion of the covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[K\left(X,X\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I\right]$
\end_inset

 has a complexity in 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(n^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 with the number 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 of training data which is impractical for large datasets and for online learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Memory Usage
\series default
:
 Storing the covariance matrix requires 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(n^{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 space,
 making GPR hard to scale to large datasets without using approximation methods (like Sparse Gaussian Processes 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bauer2016understanding"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Local Expert Agents
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Local-Expert-Agents-gpCELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous section presented the Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) and emphasized on the 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(n^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 computational complexity bottleneck that limits its application on large datasets or for online learning.
 To address these limitations,
 gpCELL applies the principle of 
\series bold
horizontal scaling
\series default
:
 rather than maintaining a single global GP,
 the system distributes the modeling task across an ensemble of local expert agents containing smaller Gaussian Processes (i.e learned with less points) that can be quickly fitted when needed.
 This section details the tripartite structrue of the local expert agents in gpCELL,
 following the architectural principles of the CELL formalism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each agent
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{A}_{i}=\left\{ \phi_{i},f_{i},\mathcal{M}_{i}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

in gpCELL is a local expert that manages a GP.
 
\series bold
The spatialization logic of kCELL is retained in gpCELL.

\series default
 Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents its area of expertise through a mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

 and a covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}$
\end_inset

.
 The activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\mapsto\left]0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 for a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

 is given by the RBF kernel value
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=\exp\left(-\frac{d\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)^{2}}{2l^{2}}\right)
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 the lengthscale of the kernel and 
\begin_inset Formula $d\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)$
\end_inset

 a measure of similarity between an agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and an input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 As in kCELL,
 
\begin_inset Formula $d\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)$
\end_inset

 is the Mahalanobis distance allowing gpCELL to remain compatible with the 
\series bold
same spatial gating mechanism as kCELL
\series default
 (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:kCELL-Context-Agents"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike the linear models used in oCELL or kCELL,
 each agent in gpCELL maintains a 
\series bold
local GP as the internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset


\series default
.
 By using a GP,
 the agent can provide not only a mean prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{*,i}$
\end_inset

 but also a predictive variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{*,i}^{2}$
\end_inset

 representing the uncertainty about the prediction for a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 such as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{i}\left(x\right)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
\mu_{*,i}\\
\sigma_{*,i}
\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
K\left(x,X_{i,\text{train}}\right)\left[K\left(X_{i,\text{train}},X_{i,\text{train}}\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I\right]^{-1}y_{i,\text{train}}\\
K\left(x,x\right)-K\left(x,X_{i,\text{train}}\right)\left[K\left(X_{i,\text{train}},X_{i,\text{train}}\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I\right]^{-1}K\left(X_{i,\text{train}},x\right)
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i,\text{train}},y_{i,\text{train}}$
\end_inset

 correspond to the feature-target dataset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 stored in its internal state 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The internal state 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 tracks a confidence value 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}\in\left[-1,1\right]$
\end_inset

 (an exponential moving average of received social feedback) that is described in detail in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:kCELL-Learning-Rules"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 and maintains an internal feature-target dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 used to retrain the internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This dataset contains the features matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i,\text{train}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{retained}}\times d}$
\end_inset

 and the target matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,\text{train}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{retained}}}$
\end_inset

.
 This dataset is managed dynamically by the agent,
 as it will be presented in the remainder of this section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As GPs predictions revert to the specified prior mean function when inputs are far from the training data support,
 due to the covariance kernel decaying with distance,
 it can lead to highly unreliable predictions and underestimation of predictive variance 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "williams2006gaussian"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The aggregation function used to build the global prediction of the ensemble of GP-based local expert agents must take this phenomenon into consideration to avoid taking into account the proposition of an unreliable agent that would pollute the final prediction.
 To ensure reliability of predictions,
 gpCELL use of 
\series bold
winner-take-all strategy
\series default
 for global prediction based on predictive confidence.
 For a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 the global prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}$
\end_inset

 is provided by the agent within the set of neighbor agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

 that reports the minimum predictive variance:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y}=f_{j}\left(x\right)\,\,\text{where}\,\,j=\arg\min_{i\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}}\left(\sigma_{*,i}^{2}\right)
\]

\end_inset

This aggregation function prioritizes the agent that is the most certain about the local structure of the data.
 That is to say,
 the selected agent is the one whose internal representation of the underlying function is best aligned with the input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Model and Shape Update
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In gpCELL,
 the update of the internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the shape of the area of expertise (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}$
\end_inset

) are intrinsically linked.
 Since the shape of the agent is obtained from the distribution of points stored in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 that is used to retrain 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 updating 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 automatically updates the area of expertise of the agent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When a new point 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x_{new},y_{new}\right)$
\end_inset

 is ingested,
 the agent must decide if or how this information should be included to its internal model.
 This is treated as a local model selection problem.
 The agent evaluates several possible configurations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Nominal
\series default
:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 remains unchanged and the current internal model is kept without modification.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Substitution
\series default
 
\series bold
of point 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset


\series default
:
 The new point replaces an existing point 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x_{j},y_{j}\right)$
\end_inset

 contained in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Augmentation
\series default
:
 The new point is added to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each configuration corresponds to a possibility of configuration for the internal dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and by extension,
 a possibility for the resulting internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 It results in a total 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\text{configurations}}$
\end_inset

 of configurations equal to:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
n_{\text{configurations}}=\underbrace{1}_{\text{Nominal}}+\underbrace{\left|\mathcal{D}_{i}\right|}_{\text{Subtitutions}}+\underbrace{1}_{\text{Augmentation}}
\]

\end_inset

To maintain computational efficiency for large memories,
 instead of evaluating all possible substitution possibilities by building a new model for each,
 heuristics can be used to select only one substitution configuration.
 Here are examples of heuristics than can be used in this context:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Random Selection
\series default
:
 A random substitution configuration is selected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Influence-Based Selection
\series default
:
 This heuristic aims at selecting the less influencial point that contributes the less to the final predictions of the GP.
 Points that are highly correlated with others in the memory of the agent tend to have low influence,
 meaning their removal would minimally affect the model's predictive performance.
 Influence can be estimated from the inverse of the covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 Smaller diagonal entries indicate points that are less influencial.
 The less influencial point 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{*}$
\end_inset

 is obtained by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
j^{*}=\arg\min_{j\in X_{i,\text{train}}}\left[K^{-1}\right]_{jj}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using such heuristic allows to reduce the number of evaluated configurations to 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
n_{\text{configurations}}=3
\]

\end_inset

 regardless of the memory size (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To chose the optimal configurations,
 the agent computes a variant of the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "akaike2003new"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for each possibility:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
AIC\left(x_{new},y_{new}\right)=\alpha\left|\mathcal{D}_{i}\right|-2\ln\left(\hat{L}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 is the weight of the number of points 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\mathcal{D}_{i}\right|$
\end_inset

 corresponds to the number of parameters representing the non-parametric complexity of the model) compared to the log-marginal likelihood (
\begin_inset Formula $\ln\left(\hat{L}\right)$
\end_inset

) of the obtained GP given the training data contained in
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 By selecting the configuration that minimizes the AIC,
 the agent performs a greedy online trade-off between predictive accuracy and model parsimony.
 When a new configuration is chosen,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is updated in consequence,
 the internal GP is retrained,
 and the shape of the area of expertise (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}$
\end_inset

) is updated from the empirical mean and covariance of the new 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i,\text{train}}$
\end_inset

.
 Finally,
 for stability reasons and to obtain a bounded complexity,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 should have a maximum size 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{max}$
\end_inset

 (beyond which point can only be swapped and not added) and a minimum size analog to the 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{ini}$
\end_inset

 in kCELL.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Computational Complexity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Considering the use of heuristics to limit the maximum number of agents that can be updated each step and to limit the number of configurations evaluated for updating those agents through the model selection process,
 and a 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(dN\right)$
\end_inset

 complexity for neighbor agent selection,
 the complexity of updates in gpCELL becomes 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
O\left(\underbrace{dN}_{\text{neighbor search}}\times\underbrace{3IN_{max}^{3}}_{\text{evaluate all config.}}\right)
\]

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is the number of iteration necessary to find the best parameters for the GP and 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the number of agents.
 As demonstrated in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 neighbors agent selection is embarrassingly parallel and thus,
 the complexity of this operation can be greatly reduced by using GPU parallelization.
 Considering an online learning context in which a classic GP is fitted on the whole dataset at every step,
 the resulting computational complexity of an update is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
O\left(In^{3}\right)
\]

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{max}\ll n$
\end_inset

,
 gpCELL is more practicable because it relies on a bounded cubic term,
 while classic GPs rely on an unbounded cubic term that scales with the number of training data.
 This simple example is limited as methods like Sparse GPs 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bauer2016understanding"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 aim at reducing the update complexity but it reveals the computational stability of gpCELL which exhibits a complexity that scales on the number of agents instead on the number of training points.
 If the embarrassingly parallel nature of neighbor search is exploited properly,
 a close-to-bounded update complexity could theoretically be reached.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Example of Single Agent Fit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To visualize the result of a learning for single local expert agent,
 a 1-dimensional example can be used.
 A small synthetic dataset is generated from the function 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x\right)=\sin\left(x\right)+\epsilon\mathcal{N}\left(0,1\right)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 the noise scaling factor that we set to 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=10^{-2}$
\end_inset

.
 Online learning is simulated by feeding the feature-target tuples (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new},y_{new}$
\end_inset

) one at a time to the agent.
 Each point is only seen once and the agent is configured with an initial memory size of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{ini}=4$
\end_inset

,
 a parameter weight 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1$
\end_inset

 and an RBF kernel with a constant prior mean 
\begin_inset Formula $m\left(x\right)=C$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Illustration-of-GP-Agent-Learning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 illustrates the result of learning a single agent on this dataset.
 Several observations about the behavior of the agent can be made:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Local Anchoring
\series default
:
 The agent does not attempt to cover the entire domain that spans over 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\left[-6,6\right]$
\end_inset

.
 It maintains its area of expertise within a localized region,
 demonstrating that the model-selection with AIC prevents the model from over-stretching to include points that would require excessive model complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Dynamic Memory Management
\series default
:
 The final set of retained points (represented as 
\emph on
red
\emph default
 dots) is larger than the initial set of training points but remains sparse over the area of expertise meaning that the agent used both 
\series bold
substitutions
\series default
 and 
\series bold
augmentations
\series default
 to optimize its internal representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Complexity-Accuract Trade-off
\series default
:
 The sparsity of the retained points suggests that the agent has selected only the most informative points for obtaining a high-likelihood model.
 This results in a parsimonious model that captures the local curvature of the underlying function.
\end_layout

