\begin_layout Paragraph
Uncertainty Quantification Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the main advantage brought by the usage of GPs in gpCELL is the ability to provide 
\series bold
predictive variance measurement
\series default
,
 which is complementary with the epistemic uncertainty estimation induced by the spatialization of local expert agents.
 Despite the guarantees of GPs to obtain a well-calibrated uncertainty quantification,
 the calibration of predictive uncertainty in the predictions of the ensemble of local expert agents in gpCELL is not mathematically guaranteed,
 and thus should be verified experimentally.
 To this end,
 the Prediction Interval Coverage Probability (PICP) is measured on the test set.
 It represents the proportion of true observations that fall inside the predicted 95% confidence interval.
 As shown in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:gpCELL-PICP"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 the PICP exceeds 0.95 for every output dimension.
 This demonstrates that gpCELL provides 
\series bold
well-calibrated intervals
\series default
,
 meaning that the true torque values fall within hte predicted 95% confidence range with high reliability.
\end_layout

