\begin_layout Section
Conclusions and Limitations
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Conclusions-and-Limitations-gpCELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This chapter detailed early exploratory works regarding the development and validation of gpCELL,
 a new instantiation of the CELL formalism designed to incorporate non-linear,
 non-parametric modeling into local expert agents through GPs.
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Gaussian-Process-Regression"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 gave an overview of what is Gaussian Process Regression and how Gaussian Processes are used in Machine Learning.
 Then,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Local-Expert-Agents-gpCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 provided a formal definition of the tripartite structure of local expert agents following the CELL formalism.
 It introduced model selection mechanism based on the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) which allows local expert agents to autonomously manage the complexity of their internal model depending on the local data manifold lying in their area of expertise.
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Learning-Rules-gpCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presented the social learning rules.
 Finally,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Online-Stationary-Modeling-gpCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presented the results of a comparative study conducted on the SARCOS benchmark dataset.
 The results demonstrated that gpCELL could achieve superior or competitive performance with other baseline algorithms such as kCELL whilc providing well-calibred uncertainty estimation on predictions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The development and evaluation of gPCELL yields valuable insights:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Online GPs
\series default
:
 By distribution the computational load across an ensemble of local expert agents,
 gpCELL bypasses the 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(N^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 bottleneck of global GPs with a horizontally scaling architecture suitable for data streams.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Uncertainty Quantification
\series default
:
 The integration of GPs as the internal model of local expert agents provides a more rigorous estimation of predictive variance in the output space which complements the epistemic uncertainty estimation derived from the spatial organization of agents.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Local Non-Linearity
\series default
:
 The experimental results show that gpCELL achieves the lowest final MAE among the compared baselines.
 It hints toward potential benefits of using non-linear internal models able to adapt their complexity based on the local data manifold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite these strengths,
 the current version of gpCELL reveals several limitations that indicate avenues for future research.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Population Overgrowth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Contrary to what we intended,
 gpCELL created more agents than its linear counterpart,
 kCELL.
 This could be linked to the weakness of the kernel spatialization approach in higher-dimensional input spaces.
 Indeed,
 when the number of input dimensions grows,
 the Mahalanobis distance progressively loses its informativeness which can lead to over-fragmentation of the input space where each local expert agent is far from all others.
 Future works should investigate dimensionality reduction or the use of more complex data distribution estimators like Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) for the spatial gating mechanism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Memory and Computational Efficiency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While gpCELL is more efficient than a global GP for online learning,
 it remains memory-intensive as it retained approximately 86% of encountered data in the memory of agents during the conducted experiment.
 A dedicated hyperparameter optimization study is needed to evaluate the trade-off between parsimony and accuracy.
 Furthermore,
 the current implementation relies on heuristics such as influence-based point selection and closest-agent only updates to maintain real-time feasability.
 These heuristics might have biased the experimental results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Social Mechanisms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike in kCELL,
 gpCELL only uses social feedbacks to install a competition for survival between agents through the update of individual confidence from social consensus estimation.
 It lacks incorporating more social mechanisms into the agent update logic.
 More advanced rules could be considered for gpCELL to make agents interact and share knowledge more actively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Better Baseline Comparison
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this chapter,
 gpCELL consists in an algorithm that adapts GPs for online learning through the CELL formalism.
 However,
 its performance have not been compared to established approximation methods like Sparse Gaussian Processes (SGPs).
 A broader study on computational and memory efficiency compared to classical GP adaptations would be interesting to consider to situate more clearly gpCELL in the state-of-the-art for GP learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Itemize
gpCELL provides a way to learn GPs online based on the CELL formalism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Agent overgrowth of gpCELL compared to kCELL could be explained by spatialization layer that do not handle complex data manifolds in input space => more advanced spatialization techniques could be envisaged to handle higher dimensional spaces and more complex local manifolds (instead of using RBF kernel) => future works on dimensionality reduction or usage of more complex data distribution estimation techniques like KDE.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Currently,
 predictive variance offered by GPs provides a more principled way of quantizing epistemic uncertainty => we have one measure in the input space directly and one measure in the output space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Memory efficiency needs to be studied => gpCELL better than a classic GP because instead of storing one big cov matrix it stores a lot of small ones => however,
 86% of the dataset have been retained => A hyperopt study could be interesting to measure the impact of the AIC model selection process with different 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 to evaluate the tradeoff between number of retained points and efficiency (if there is one)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Need more studies to study the growth in the number of agents => for now it seems that there is an overgrowth in the number of agents
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Need more studies on the calibration of the global model => can uncertainty be trusted ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Need comparison with classic GPs or a minima SGPs 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Computational efficiency of the algo better than classical GPs for large datasets,
 however still needs improvement because currently we cannot use this approach to its maximum potential due to computational limitations (cf heuristics that we use for implemntation)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Memory efficiency not on point for now => competitive performances but need for better memory efficiency => work on that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The chosen heuristics (for implementation) used to make the algo run in reasonable time could have biased the results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Maybe the prediction method by selecting the most confident agent could be extended to the neighbors => could be studied
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Social mechanisms could be leveraged to make agents interact more with each other
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Hyperopt of the kernel parameters can give variable results because some solvers (for hyperopt) use randomness
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

