\begin_layout Enumerate
[Industrial Requirements for Speed Recommendation Systems] What are the Industrial Requirements for Intelligent Speed Recommendation ?
 => Begin by recalling that initially ISA systems were just supposed to inform the driver about the speed limit (as specified in the EU regulations it's the minimal requirement) => But in reality users do not drive at the maximum legal speed all the time depending on road conditions (e.g traffic,
 weather,
 ...) => With modern vehicle connectivity more data can be gathered in real time about the road conditions => Allow for more intelligent systems to advise drivers
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Multi-Objective Optimization => Need to provide an added value (ex:
 optimize fuel consumption can be very profitable for both fleet managers and individuals) => but objectives can be conflicting with each others
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Reduction of Energy Consumption (
\begin_inset Formula $\neq$
\end_inset

 for eletric vehicles and thermic vehicles)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Reduction of Travel Time
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Safety and Legal Guarantees => Need that recommendation be constrained by legal regulation and safety requirements
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Legal Regulations => Speed Limits
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Safety => Distance to preceding vehicle,
 speed in turns to avoid drifting,
 speed in difficult weather conditions (eg.
 to avoid aquaplanning in the rain or skidding on ice)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Driver Acceptance => driver can refuse,
 delay or apply a speed recommendation => but user must keep authority on his vehicle (Overridability which justify recommendation)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Can be achieved through more or less invasive means => from indication on the vehicle dashboard to haptic resistance on the accelerator pedal
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Robustness to highly uncertain and diverse environments (even to the rarest events)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Many heterogeneous traffic participants (cars,
 trucks,
 pedestrians,
 etc...) with hard-to-predict behavior
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Drivers drive very differently in urban or rural environments,
 when it rains or snow,
 when there is a traffic jam or not,
 etc...
 => Not all driving contexts are equally frequent
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Certifiability => Definition:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Certifiability for ADAS (Advanced Driver Assistance Systems) refers to the process of proving that these safety-critical systems meet rigorous regulatory,
 safety,
 and performance standards to gain formal approval for deployment in vehicles.
 It ensures systems like automatic emergency braking or lane-keeping assist operate reliably without introducing undue risks.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Certifiability goes beyond basic testing by requiring comprehensive evidence of functional safety,
 cybersecurity,
 and intended functionality across diverse scenarios.
 It addresses the complexity of ADAS,
 where sensors,
 AI algorithms,
 and actuators interact in unpredictable real-world conditions,
 demanding standards like ISO 26262 (functional safety) and ISO/PAS 21448 (SOTIF for safety of intended functionality).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Functional Safety
\series default
 (ISO 26262):
 Systems must achieve Automotive Safety Integrity Levels (ASIL) through hazard analysis,
 risk assessment (HARA),
 and traceability from requirements to tests,
 including hardware/software audits and fault-tolerant design.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Scenario Validation
\series default
:
 Extensive testing in simulations,
 hardware-in-the-loop (HIL),
 closed tracks,
 and real-world conditions covers operational design domains (ODDs),
 edge cases,
 corner cases,
 and environmental stressors like fog or high traffic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Performance and Compliance
\series default
:
 Features must meet UN regulations (e.g.,
 UN R171 for driver control assistance systems) with KPIs for metrics like time-to-collision,
 braking profiles,
 driver monitoring,
 and human-machine interfaces.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Process and Lifecycle Audits
\series default
:
 Parallel certification of development processes,
 production conformity,
 software updates (including OTA),
 and end-of-life procedures,
 with documentation for type approval authorities.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
