reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 demonstrated that oCELL and kCELL exhibited introspective properties that could be linked to the traditional notions of explainability and interpretability.
 Moreover,
 when using white box models for agents the system can be considered as a white box.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
[Synthesis and Conclusion] What remains to be done for real-world deployment ?
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
[
\series bold
Certifiability
\series default
] Need for accurate simulation environments for the development process:
 for pretraining (because online learning algos may take time to converge so a pretraining default model that will be able to adapt to the driver afterwards allow the system to work with minimal guarantees quickly) and validation (need to assess theoretical guarantees beforehand to validate the stability of the learning behavior on edge cases)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
hard to develop simulations for speed recommendation as it is almost mandatory to have a complex driver behavior models that mimicks the behavior of a human driver
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
much harder than building accurate fuel consumption models or accurate rigid body simulations even it's also important to bridge the gap between simulation and reality (Sim-to-Real Gap)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
[
\series bold
Safety
\series default
] => In Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 we demonstrate that kCELL is differentiable and more importantly locally linearizable => can be integrated with QP solvers for local optimization like Sequential Quadratic Programming => ge
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
[
\series bold
Safety
\series default
] Still lack of mathematical proof for safety guarantees and hard constraints => introspective properties allow to increase the reliability of the model but this statement rely on empirical observations rather than mathematical proof
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
[
\series bold
Certifiability
\series default
] => Could be interesting to study Lyapunov-based formal certification for CELL systems to get guarantees about stability of optimization.
 => In the thesis we went from unknown risk to measured risk and future directions are to target zero risk 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
[
\series bold
Certifiability
\series default
] Certifiability remains a significantly harder challenge when including continuously learning components as in the proposed architecture => requirements that the learning stays bounded and has mathematical guarantees about convergence => which is lacking in the developed CELL systems such as oCELL and kCELL
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Runtime adaptation also conflicts with Safety of the Intended Functionality (SOTIF,
 ISO/PAS 21448),
 as known safe behaviors may degrade without retriggering full validation.
 Regulatory bodies like UNECE demand evidence that learning stays within bounded operational design domains (ODDs),
 preventing "concept drift" from real-world data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Our learning model is non-parametric => complexity hard to estimate in practice => depend on amount of data encountered => add uncertainty at this level
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
kCELL still struggles with higher dimensional inputs and it does not handle properly categorical inputs => In road contexts,
 the input space can comprise continuous variables and categorical ones => currently kCELL only handles continuous variables => need to develop CELL instantations derived from kCELL that would be able to handle categorical variables properly
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
[
\series bold
Acceptance
\series default
] => our initial assumption about driver acceptance handling through online learning may be partially wrong if the model lacks some information about the driver to model his behavior => Could be interesting to add input variables related to driver state (tired,
 angry,
 etc...) to allow for better personalization.
 => to summarize:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

To achieve the next level of personalization,
 the CELL agents should not only observe the vehicle's reaction but also the driver's internal state (tired,
 stressed,
 distracted)
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_inset


