\begin_layout Section
Industrial Requirements for Speed Recommendation Systems
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Industrial-Requirements"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The technical evolution of speed recommendation systems such as Intelligent Speed Assistance (ISA) has historically followed a trajectory from passive information retrieval to inform the driver about the current legal speed limit 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ehrlichLAVIAFrenchISA2003,driscollLaviaEvaluationPotential2007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 to active,
 more intelligent recommendations.
 Initially,
 research and industrial efforts were primarily focused on the robust identification of legal speed limits leveraging digital maps and accurate GPS positioning 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bahlmannMultimodalSpeedLimit2008,danielFusingNavigationVision2014,danielMultilevelDempsterShaferSpeed2012"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The primary goal was informational,
 to ensure the driver is always aware of the current legal constraint.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As ISA is becoming manatory due to EU regulations 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "etsc2017isa,etsc2018isa,eu2019regulation,eu2021delegated"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 modern vehicles are no longer isolated agents but connected entities capable of gathering and processing a large amount of real-time data regarding traffic density,
 weather conditions or road surface quality,
 etc...
 This shift allows for a transition from static speed limit advisory to intelligent speed recommendation systems advising an optimal speed profile accounting for the dynamic complexity of the road context.
 As the industrial scope of speed recommendation expands,
 the deployment of such intelligent systems in a commercial vehicle requires satisfying a rigorous set of requirements which are categorized in the remainder of this section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Multi-Objective Optimization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike purely advisory ISA systems,
 intelligent speed recommendation acts as a high-level controller that must solve a multi-objective optimization problem.
 The recommendation must provide a clear added value to the stakeholders (e.g fleet managers or individual drivers).
 This requires the formulation of a cost function that negotiates between conflicting objectives.
 Here are some examples:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Energy Efficiency
\series default
:
 Minimizing fuel consumption for thermic vehicles or optimizing energy recovery through regenerative braking and discharge for eletric vehicles (EVs).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Temporal Efficiency
\series default
:
 Reducing travel time while maintaining a smooth speed profile.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Comfort
\series default
:
 Avoiding aggressive longitudinal maneuvers that could degrade the passenger experience or increase the cognitive load of the driver.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Safety And Legal Guarantees
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Speed recommendations must be strictly bounded by physical and legal constraints.
 These act as hard state constraints:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Legal Regulations
\series default
:
 Ensuring the recommended speed profile never violates legal speed limits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Safety
\series default
:
 Accounting for the dynamic road context to prevent reaching dangerous states:
 such as avoiding excessive speed in curves to avoid drifting,
 maintaining safe inter-vehicle distances and adjusting for degraded road friction to prevent aquaplaning or skidding.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Overridability and Driver Acceptance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As specified in the European regulation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "eu2021delegated"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the human remains the final authority over the recommendation system.
 Furthermore,
 as speed recommendation can be formulated as a HRC problem (cf.
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Introduction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 driver acceptance is a major factor determining the efficiency of the recommendation system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Overridability
\series default
:
 As the driver maintains full authority on his vehicle,
 the speed is recommended and not enforced.
 This differentiate the speed recommendation problem from the speed control problem in autonomous driving.
 The driver should be able to refuse,
 delay or accept the suggested speed recommendation at any time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Acceptance
\series default
:
 Acceptance is influenced by how the recommendation is calculated and how it is communuicated to the driver.
 This can be achieved through more or less invasive Human-Machine Interfaces (HMI) ranging from passive visual indications on the vehicle dashboard to active haptic resistances on the accelerator pedal 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "zhaoHowDoesMural2022,ehrlichLAVIAFrenchISA2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 the objective is to recommend a plausible speed set point and to communicate it in the best possible way to the driver.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Robustnes to Uncertainty and Diversity of Road Contexts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The recommendation system should maintain its efficiency across a diverse range of Operational Design Domains (ODDs) characterized by high environmental and behavioral variability:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Contextual Diversity
\series default
:
 Drivers and vehicles operate in highly different conditions (e.g.
 urban vs rural,
 varying weather conditions,
 traffic congestions).
 Not all driving contexts are equally frequent,
 requiring the system to handle edge cases or rare events with the same reliability as standard scenarios.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Heterogeneous Traffic Participants
\series default
:
 The presence of other road users such as cars,
 trucks,
 motorcycles or even pedestrians,
 that adopt hard-to-predict behaviors introduces significant stochasticity into the environment,
 requiring robust predictive capabilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Certifiability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In an industrial context,
 certifiability can be defined as following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Certifiability refers to the formal process of proving that safety-critical systems meet rigorous regulatory,
 safety and performance standards to gain approval for real-world deployment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
In the automotive industry Advanced Driver Assistance Systems (ADAS) are subject to several requirements regarding certifiability such as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Functional Safety
\series default
 (ISO 26262 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ISO26262-1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
 Systems must achieve specific Automotive Safety Integrity Levels (ASIL) through Hazard Analysis and Risk Assessment (HARA) and maintain full traceability from requirements to testing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Safety of the Intended Functionality
\series default
 (SOTIF - ISO/PAS 21448 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ISO21448-2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
 This addresses the complexity of ADAS where sensors and algorithms must interact reliably in unpredictable real-world conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Scenario Validation
\series default
:
 Deployment requires comprehensive evidence gathered through simulations,
 Hardware-in-the-Loop (HIL) testing and real-world track trials to validate performance against United Nations (UN) regulations (e.g.
 UN R171 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "UNR171"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) for driver assistance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Data Integrity and Cybersecurity
\series default
 (ISO/SAE 21434 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ISO-SAE-21434-2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
 As modern vehicles leverage V2X (Vehicle-to-Everything) connectivity to gather road data,
 the recommendation system must be robust against corrupted or malicious data inputs that could lead to dangerous speed recommendations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Deterministic Performance
\series default
:
 Alongside functional safety,
 embedded automotive systems require strict guarantees on computational resource usage.
 Algorithms must exhibit Worst-Case Execution Times (WCET) to ensure they meet the real-time constraints of the vehicle's Electronic Control Units (ECUs).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Proposed Architecture
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Proposed-Architecture"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous section exposed a comprehensive set of industrial requirements for the deployment of intelligent speed recommendation systems in real-world conditions.
 It ranged from multi-objective optimization to strict certifiability constraints.
 While significant progress have been made for automated speed control in the context of fully autonomous driving,
 speed recommendation remains a distinct challenge as it includes the human driver in the loop.
 This section introduces the architecture proposed in this thesis to address the speed recommendation problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Distinguishing Recommendation and Control
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As exposed in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Introduction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 a core distinction must be made between speed control and speed recommendation.
 In the context of autonomous driving,
 speed control aims at replacing the human driver,
 operating in a closed-loop where the system possesses direct authority over the vehicle's actuators such as the accelerator and brake pedals.
 Conversely,
 in speed recommendation,
 the humans retains full authority over the vehicle.
 In this context,
 the human acts as a biological actuator to the system (falling into the shared controllability challenge exposed in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:State-of-the-Art"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 The recommendation system's output is not a control signal directly applied to the vehicle's low level controls,
 but is a suggested set-point that the driver may choose to refuse,
 delay or accept.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite its importance for road safety and energy efficiency,
 speed recommendation has been significantly less studied than speed control.
 Most research efforts have been focused on perfecting the 
\emph on
robot
\emph default
 side of the problem,
 often neglecting the complex modeling of the 
\emph on
human
\emph default
 side required for effective recommendation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To highlight the specific research gap of the literature about speed control and recommendation,
 we re-evaluate the body of literature introduced in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Introduction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 by applying the technical taxonomies established in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:State-of-the-Art"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 This classification allows us to visualize the current state of the art regarding control and recommendation strategies (in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Paper-Classification-Speed-Recommendation-Strategies"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 and predictive modeling through learning approaches (in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Paper-Classification-Speed-Recommendation-Learning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 It should be specified that Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Paper-Classification-Speed-Recommendation-Learning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 does not include any online learning method as the ones presented in this thesis,
 that are learning continuously a model of the environment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As shown in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Paper-Classification-Speed-Recommendation-Strategies"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 there is a clear absence of research works exploring the combination between Online Learning approaches and MPC.
 Furthermore,
 Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Paper-Classification-Speed-Recommendation-Learning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 reveals that the majority of research relies on global parametric models with no existing works,
 to our knowledge,
 about using a 
\series bold
continuously learning,
 local non-parametric approaches
\series default
 to model the dynamics of the driving context.
\end_layout

