\begin_layout Standard
The choice of an MPC-based architecture including a CELL online learning predictive model is motivated by its ability to address the industrial requirements identified in the previous section within a unified mathematical framework.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Multi-Objective 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The choice of the MPC framework naturally addresses the Multi-Objective requirement.
 By formulating a cost function that weights energy efficiency,
 travel time and comfort,
 the system can produce a speed recommendation that represents the optimal tradeoff for a specific driving context.
 MPC with the right solver allows for the simultaneous negotiation of conflicting goals within a classic optimization process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Safety and Legal Constraints
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Safety-critical systems require hard guarantees.
 Within the MPC loop,
 Safety and Legal constraints requirements are handled as hard state and input constraints evaluated over the trajectory predicted by the CELL model.
 Using appropriate solvers within MPC ensures that the recommended speed profile is inherently compliant with legal speed limits and physical safety criteria (e.g.
 curvature-based speed limits or maximum inter-vehicle distances,
 ...).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Certifiability and Computational Predictability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A major challenge for the deployment of learning-based ADAS is the requirement for Worst-Case Execution Times (WCET) and bounded computational complexity (ISO 26262 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ISO26262-1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 One of the primary advantages of the proposed architecture is that MPC allows the use of battle-tested industrial grade solvers (such as OSQP 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "stellato2020osqp"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 or qpOASES 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ferreau2014qpoases"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) that are compliant with these requirements.
 This ensures that the optimization process finished within the strict real-time deadlines of embedded automotive hardware.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Driver Acceptance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A core assumption of this thesis is the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{assumption}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Driver Acceptance is intrinsically linked to how well the system's recommendations align with the driver's own behavior.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{assumption}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the context of HRC,
 the machine should include a model of its human partner's intents to better synergize 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dani2024human"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Given the previously defined assumption,
 we assume that if the predictive model accurately captures the specific driving style and tendencies of the driver,
 the recommendations generated will be perceived as more natural and will be more readily accepted.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Continuous online learning via the CELL instantiations allows the recommendation system to personalize the predictive model at deployment time.
 By adapting to the individual's non-stationary behavior and specific environmental reactions,
 the recommendation system minimizes the model-mismatch with the driver,
 representing a step toward a trust-based collaborative system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Towards Personalized,
 Safe and Trustworthy Speed Recommendation
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Towards-Personalized-Safe-Trustworthy-Speed-Recommendation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous section presented the architectural choice made in this thesis to addres the speed recommendation problem.
 This section demonstrates how the specific theoretical and algorithmic contributions of this thesis directly address some of the industrial requirements listed in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Industrial-Requirements"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Safety through Epistemic Uncertainty Estimation and Introspection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A major risk that can occur when deploying a learned predictive model in safety-critical systems is their Out-of-Distribution (OoD) behavior.
 Some types of learned models such as neural networks tend to be overconfident 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "guo2017calibration"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 even in unknown regions of the state-space,
 leading to dangerous recommendations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As demonstrated in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 the CELL instantiations and more specifically the kCELL system mitigate this issue by leveraging the spatialization of local expert agents to improve the reliability of the learned predictive model in a MPC setting.
 Because each agent is responsible for a localized region of the input space,
 the system can estimate espistemic uncertainty based on the value of activation of the agents.
 This allows the system to 
\emph on
know when it does not know.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From an industrial perspective,
 this introspection allows for the implementation of safety guardrails.
 When the recommendation system detects high epistemic uncertainty,
 it can trigger the use of a fallback strategy (e.g.
 reverting to a simple legal speed limit advisory system) instead of providing a potentially erroneous or dangerous recommendation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Certifiability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The requirement for Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET) is crucial when working with a limited embedded hardware configuration.
 In Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we demonstrated that the kCELL was fully compatible with gradient-based high-performance Quadratic Programming (QP) solvers through its local linearization capabilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By integrating kCELL with high-performance solvers such as OSQP or qpOASES within an MPC loop,
 we provided a path to certifiability.
 These solvers offer strong mathematical guarantees regarding bounded computational complexity and convergence.
 This ensures that even with a sophisticated,
 continuously learning model,
 the speed recommendation is calculated within strict real-time constraints.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Contextual Integrity and Parameter Isolation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In terms of certifiability,
 a conflict exists between the need for continuous online learning in our case and the static,
 verifiable designs required by ISO 26262 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ISO26262-1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Global parametric learning systems (e.g.
 neural networks),
 when learning online,
 can often be unpredictable as an update can lead to unpredictable regressions in unrelated scenarios because virtually,
 any parameter of the model can change (cf.
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:State-of-the-Art"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 This a phenomenon known as catastrophic forgetting.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The CELL systems mitigate this issue through Parameter Isolation.
 Because the learning is local,
 updates to an agent specialized in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Highway driving
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 do not alter the behavior of agents specialized in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Urban driving
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 because the corresponding subsets of local expert agents are spatially separable in the input space (as illustrated in Figure ).
 This property provides 
\series bold
Contextual Integrity
\series default
 to the continuously learning predictive model.
 It ensure that the model used for speed recommendations is always the one learned in the corresponding driving context.
\end_layout

