\begin_layout Paragraph
Trust through Transparency and Interpretability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 the industrial requirement for Driver Acceptance is addressed through the white-box nature of the CELL systems when they include white box models as the internal model of local expert agents.
 As discussed in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 the spatial organization of agents provides introspective properties that can be linked to the traditional notions of explainability and interpretability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike global models,
 the inner workings of a CELL system can be inspected.
 One can identify exactly which local expert is responsible for a prediction and in which driving context it was trained on.
 This transparency is important for providing the rationale behind a speed recommendation if requested by a certification authority or the user.
 For instance,
 feature importance can be studied easily when using linear regressions or a faulty badly learned agent can be easily identified to be corrected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Synthesis and Conclusion
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Synthesis-and-Conclusion-Industrial"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the previous sections,
 we established the industrial requirements for intelligent speed recommendation and demonstrated how the properties of the CELL systems direclty contribute towards fulfilling these standards.
 However,
 the path toward full industrial deployment remains open for our proposed architecture.
 This section exposes the remaining challenges and outlines future research directions necessary to bridge the gap between the current algorithmic foundations of our approach and a market-ready ADAS system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
The Simulation and Validation Gap
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A primary requisite for certifiability is the existence of high-fidelity simulation environments for both pre-training and validation of speed recommendation strategies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While CELL systems are tailored for online adaptation,
 they should include a cold start initialization.
 In a commercial setting,
 a system must provide minimal performance from the moment it is activated.
 This necessitates a pre-trained default model capable of working before being able to adapt to the driver.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Moreover,
 developing such simulation environment for speed recommendation is significantly more complex than standard speed control environments.
 While rigid-body vehicle dynamics and fuel consumption models are governed by well-defined physical laws,
 driver behavior is governed by stochastic psychological processes,
 making it a much harder target for simulation.
 It requries the elaboration of sophisticated driver behavior models that accurately mimic the behavior of various human drivers.
 Indeed,
 with a proper driver behavior model,
 the stability of long-term learning could be assessed across the entire Operational Design Domain (ODD),
 including rare edge cases that are too dangerous to test on open roads,
 to allow for the certifiability of the recommendation system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Toward Formal Safety and Convergence Proofs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While we demonstrated in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 that kCELL is locally linearizable and can be successfully integrated into QP-based optimization loops,
 the current safety guarantees remain mainly empirical.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The introspective properties of kCELL allow to move from 
\emph on
unknown risk
\emph default
 to 
\emph on
measured risk 
\emph default
by quantifying epistemic uncertainty from the spatial organization of the local expert agents.
 However,
 industrial certification ultimately requires more steps to reach a 
\emph on
close to zero risk
\emph default
 status through mathematical proofs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Future works should explore Lyapunov-based formal certifications for CELL systems to provide rigorous guarantees regarding the stability of the optimization process.
 This is particularly challenging for continuously learning predictive models because the model parameters are varying constantly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because kCELL is essentially non-parametric,
 its complexity depends on the volume of encountered data and on the complexity of the function to approximate.
 Future works should also seek to find mathematical bounds on learning convergence and resource usage for such evolving architectures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Handling More Diverse Data Sources
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The current instantiations of CELL currently face technical limitations regarding the type of inputs they can handle.
 Real-world driving contexts involve a mix of continuous variables (e.g speed,
 distance) and categorical variables (e.g weather types,
 road signs,
 traffic light states).
 Currently,
 the developed CELL systems have been designed for continuous input spaces.
 Designing novel CELL systems able to handle heterogeneous categorical data is essential for robust and accurate environmental perception of driving contexts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to that,
 as the number of input dimensions grows,
 the spatialization of agents suffers from the curse of dimensionality.
 Thus,
 future research directions on CELL systems should focus on finding new ways to spatialize local expert agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
More In-Depth Personalization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 the core assumption made in the context of this thesis about driver acceptance need for refinement.
 While modeling the driver-vehicle dynamical system as a whole is a step forward efficient speed recommendation systems,
 a driver's behavior is deeply influenced by internal states such as fatigue,
 stress or distraction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To achieve a higher level of trust and acceptance,
 future iterations of the proposed speed recommendation architecture should incorporate physiological or behavioral indicators of the driver's state.
 By knowing if a driver is angry or tired,
 the predictive model is no longer subject to uncertainty induced by hidden variables.
\end_layout

