General Conclusion
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:General-Conclusion"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section provides a retrospective summary of the research work presented in this thesis.
 It details how each chapter contributed toward building safe and adaptive systems capable of synergizing with humans.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Problem Definition and Motivations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Introduction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 began defining the industrial context and motivation of this research work related to the development of Intelligent Speed Recommendation.
 A distinction has been made between 
\series bold
speed control
\series default
,
 i.e automated driving,
 and 
\series bold
speed recommendation
\series default
.

\series bold
 
\series default
As this distinction emphasizes on handling the human in the loop,
 it led to the abstraction of the speed recommendation problem into a Human-Robot Collaboration (HRC) problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This chapter argued that for such system to be effective,
 it must possess two essential capabilities:
 the ability to continuously learn and adpat to the non-stationary,
 uncertain,
 intent of the driver;
 and the ability to exploir that model reliably within a control framework.
 This allowed to identify two primary research axes:
 
\series bold
online learning of non-stationary dynamics
\series default
 an 
\series bold
reliability in learning-based control
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Literature Review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The literature review presented in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:State-of-the-Art"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 allowed to position this thesis work within the state of the art.
 By analyzing the challenges of optimal control with learned models,
 
\series bold
Model Predictive Control (MPC)
\series default
 have been identified as the most suitable framework for HRC due to its theoretical ability to handle constraints and integrate a continuously updating predictive model.
 Regarding the machine learning research axis,
 
\series bold
Catastrophic Forgetting
\series default
 has been identified as a major challenge for online adaptation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Local learning algorithms
\series default
 have been deemed promising to address this challenge.
 These learning methods isolate parameters through spatial decomposition of the learning problem,
 offering a structural solution to the Catastrophic Forgetting problem.
 This led to the exploration of a specific research niche:
 
\series bold
self-organizing multi-agent systems for local learning
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
The CELL formalism and its Instantiations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To address the online learning problem,
 the Context Ensemble Local Learning (CELL) formalism has been introduced in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 It provides a 
\series bold
framework for designing online learning algorithms
\series default
 by treating the learning as a 
\series bold
social process
\series default
 where local expert agents self-organize to represent the the data manifold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A first instantiation have been proposed:
 
\series bold
oCELL
\series default
.
 It uses a spatialization of local expert agents based on orthotopes.
 It allowed to demonstrate competitive performance with state of the art learning algorithms on a simple 2D benchmark.
 The development and empirical validation of oCELL,
 also allowed to identify emerging introspective properties that can be derived from the result of the spatial organization of local expert agents,
 and that can be linked to the traditional notions of explainability and interpretability in machine learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In response to the limitations of oCELL,
 a new instantiation of CELL have been developed:
 
\series bold
kCELL
\series default
.
 Instead of using orthotopes for the spatialization of agents it uses a smooth,
 continuous approach based on kernels.
 It also introduced more sophisticated social rules that allowed to make the system less dependent on some hard-to-tune hyperparameters.
 It proved capable of adapting to non-stationary environments while maintaining a transparent spatialization of knowledge and being able to compete with state-of-the-art online learning algorithms in terms of performance on a inverse modeling task.
 The goal behind the developed of kCELL was to build upon oCELL to build a learning algorithm more suited for being integrated into MPC.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Integration into the Control Loop
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a robust learning algorithm has been designed through kCELL,
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 addresses the exploitation problem.
 It has been demonstrated that kCELL could be implemented efficiently for real-time applications using GPU acceleration and geospatial indexing.
 The core contribution of this chapter was showing that the 
\series bold
introspective properties
\series default
 of the kCELL ensemble of local expert agents could be leveraged to improve the learning of the predictive model within a control loop by guiding 
\series bold
exploration
\series default
 toward unknown but relevant regions;
 and to improve the reliability of the resulting model by safeguarding the 
\series bold
exploitation
\series default
,
 discouraging the MPC optimizer from selecting Out-of-Distribution (OoD) trajectories that lie in unknown regions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Enhancing the Robustness of Learning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While kCELL provides a measure of epistemic uncertainty from the spatial organization of local expert agents in the 
\series bold
input space
\series default
,
 it does not provide an uncertainty measure on the predictions of local models in the 
\series bold
output space
\series default
.
 Recognizing this need for more formal uncertainty quantification for robust control,
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Scalable-Non-Linear-CELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 
\series bold
returned to the modeling layer
\series default
 to explore the 
\series bold
modularity
\series default
 of the CELL systems regarding the model class of internal models of local expert agents.
 Thus,
 gpCELL has been introduced where local expert agents use 
\series bold
Gaussian Processes (GP) as internal models
\series default
.
 This allowed the ensemble of local expert agents to behave like an 
\series bold
heterogeneous ensemble
\series default
,
 as agents can dynamically 
\series bold
adapt their complexity
\series default
 to the local data manifold.
 Through empirical validation,
 gpCELL provided well-calibrated predictive variance interval on predictions while maintaining the computational advantages of the local learning approach over traditional GP in an online learning task.
 These confidence intervals can prove essential for safety-critical control using learned models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Industrial Synthesis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Industrial-use-Case"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 closed by linking the scientific contributions of this thesis back to the original industrial requirements,
 evaluating how the proposed architecture,
 from the CELL formalism to the introspective MPC loop,
 addressed the specific challenges of driver acceptance,
 safety and energy efficiency.
 This synthesis allowed to identify the remaining gaps between our current research and a production-ready ssystem,
 setting the stage for future works.
\end_layout

